---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import SocialSizes from '../tools/SocialSizes';
import { getTool } from '../data/tools';
import { PRESETS, VERIFIED_ON, presetsByPlatform } from '../data/presets';

const tool = getTool('social-media-image-sizes');
const socialGroups = presetsByPlatform('social');
const marketGroups = presetsByPlatform('marketplace');

/* Headings this page adds through the `content` slot. The layout cannot
   see inside a slot, so the rail's table of contents is declared here or
   it silently goes out of date. */
const extraToc = [
  { id: "reference", label: "The full size reference" },
  { id: "youtube-note", label: "The YouTube thumbnail advice you have read is out of date" },
  { id: "one-image", label: "Why one image for everything does not work" },
  { id: "source", label: "Start from the largest original you have" },
];
---

<ToolPage extraToc={extraToc} tool={tool}>
  <SocialSizes slot="tool" client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="section section--wide">
      <h2 id="reference">The full size reference</h2>
      <p>
        Every size the tool can produce, in one table. Last checked on <strong>{VERIFIED_ON}</strong>.
        Platforms change these without announcement, so confirm anything critical against the
        platform's own documentation before a launch.
      </p>

      <h3>Social platforms</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Platform</th>
              <th>Placement</th>
              <th>Size</th>
              <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              Array.from(socialGroups.entries()).flatMap(([platform, presets]) =>
                presets.map((preset, i) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{i === 0 ? platform : ''}</td>
                    <td>{preset.placement}</td>
                    <td class="mono">{preset.width} x {preset.height}</td>
                    <td>{preset.note ?? ''}</td>
                  </tr>
                )),
              )
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Marketplaces</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Platform</th>
              <th>Placement</th>
              <th>Size</th>
              <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              Array.from(marketGroups.entries()).flatMap(([platform, presets]) =>
                presets.map((preset, i) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{i === 0 ? platform : ''}</td>
                    <td>{preset.placement}</td>
                    <td class="mono">{preset.width} x {preset.height}</td>
                    <td>{preset.note ?? ''}</td>
                  </tr>
                )),
              )
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="youtube-note">The YouTube thumbnail advice you have read is out of date</h2>
      <p>
        Almost every guide online still says a YouTube thumbnail should be 1280 by 720 with a
        hard 2 MB limit. Both halves of that have moved.
      </p>
      <p>
        YouTube's own documentation now recommends <strong>3840 by 2160</strong> at 16:9, with a
        minimum width of 640 pixels. And the file size ceiling depends on where you upload from:
        <strong>2 MB from mobile</strong>, but <strong>50 MB from desktop</strong>. If you have
        been fighting the 2 MB limit, you were uploading from your phone — the same thumbnail
        goes up untouched from a computer.
      </p>
      <p>
        1280 by 720 still works and is still accepted. It is simply no longer what YouTube asks
        for, and on a large modern display the difference is visible. Both sizes are in the
        preset list so you can pick deliberately.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="one-image">Why one image for everything does not work</h2>
      <p>
        Each placement crops to its own aspect ratio. Upload a square to something expecting 16:9
        and the platform decides what to remove — and its automatic crop has no idea which part
        of your image matters. That is how you end up with a listing photo missing the product,
        or a thumbnail with the top of somebody's head cut off.
      </p>
      <p>
        Generating each size deliberately means you choose the framing. The tool centres its crop
        automatically, which is right most of the time, but check the previews. When the
        automatic framing gets it wrong on an important image, use the{' '}
        <a href="/crop-image/">crop tool</a> to frame that one by hand.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="source">Start from the largest original you have</h2>
      <p>
        Every output is derived from your source, so the source sets the ceiling. Scaling down
        looks excellent; scaling up cannot invent detail and always looks soft.
      </p>
      <p>
        The largest common target here is 2048 pixels for Shopify products, followed by 2000 for
        Etsy listings and 3840 for a YouTube thumbnail. If your source is smaller than the sizes
        you have selected, the tool tells you which ones will be enlarged rather than quietly
        producing something soft.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a platform publishes a hard upload limit — YouTube's 2 MB mobile cap being the
        notable one — the tool searches for the highest quality that fits underneath it rather
        than handing you a file that will be rejected.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>
